7} < 6$ (both positive), giving $a + b\\sqrt{7} < 6b$, i.e., $|a + b\\sqrt{7}| = a + b\\sqrt{7} < 6b$.\n\nAnd $|a - b\\sqrt{7}| = b \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a}{b} - \\sqrt{7}\\right| < b \\cdot \\frac{2}{b^4} = \\frac{2}{b^3}$.\n\nSo $|a^2 - 7b^2| = |a - b\\sqrt{7}| \\cdot |a + b\\sqrt{7}| < \\frac{2}{b^3} \\cdot 6b = \\frac{12}{b^2}$.\n\nSince $a^2 - 7b^2$ is a nonzero integer (nonzero because $\\sqrt{7}$ is irrational, so $a^2 \\neq 7b^2$), we have $|a^2 - 7b^2| \\ge 1$.\n\nThus $1 \\le \\frac{12}{b^2}$, so $b^2 \\le 12$, giving $b \\le 3$ (since $b$ is a positive integer and $b \\ge 2$, we get $b \\in \\{2, 3\\}$).\n\nThen check each case as above.\n\nThis is a complete and clean solution. Let me write it up.\n"
